### [Copy link to heading](https://nextjs.org/blog/next-16-4#smaller-production-bundles) Smaller production bundles
In 16.4, Turbopack generates shorter CSS Module class names in production, reducing the size of stylesheets and the HTML and JavaScript that reference them.

Additionally, Turbopack now uses export mangling to further reduce bundle size by shortening the internal JavaScript export names used to connect modules.

## [Copy link to heading](https://nextjs.org/blog/next-16-4#new-experimental-features) New experimental features
### [Copy link to heading](https://nextjs.org/blog/next-16-4#worker-threads) Worker threads
Turbopack runs tools like Babel, PostCSS, and webpack loaders in separate Node.js processes that communicate with it over sockets.

### [Copy link to heading](https://nextjs.org/blog/next-16-4#additional-roots-and-global-virtual-store-support) Additional roots and global virtual store support
```
import path from 'node:path';
import type { NextConfig } from 'next';

const nextConfig: NextConfig = {
  experimental: {
    turbopackAdditionalRoots: {
      linkedPackages: { path: path.join(__dirname, '../packages') },
    },
  },
};

export default nextConfig;
```

